NAME
    mv - rename or move a file

SYNOPSIS
    mv [-f] <src> <dst>

DESCRIPTION
    Renames <src> to <dst>, moving it between directories if needed. Paths
    resolve against the current directory.

    If <dst> is an existing DIRECTORY, <src> moves into it and keeps its
    basename (mv notes.txt backup/), as real mv does.

    If <dst> is an existing FILE, mv REFUSES unless -f is given:

        mv: /B.TXT exists (use -f to replace)

    With -f the file is replaced and its contents are gone. This differs from
    POSIX mv, which replaces silently; the choice is deliberate on a system
    with no undo, no trash and no snapshots. cp behaves the same way.

    It is a refusal and not a prompt, on purpose. Asking needs the keyboard,
    and mv has none when it runs as a pipeline stage, under cpu on another
    machine, or when the rename arrives from a 9P peer - so a prompt would
    work in the interactive case and hang or fail everywhere else.

    The check is made against the name that would actually be replaced, so
    `mv a dir` reports dir/a rather than dir.

    Both must be ordinary files: replacing a directory, or moving a directory
    onto an existing name, is refused with "already exists" whatever flags are
    given. Only -f is accepted, and only before the operands; any other -word
    is an error rather than a filename.

    On ext2 the replacement is very nearly atomic. The whole change is one
    write of the destination's directory entry, re-pointing it at the source's
    inode, so the name never resolves to nothing - a reader either sees the old
    file or the new one. FAT32 and exFAT cannot do this: a directory entry
    there holds the file's location itself rather than an inode number, so the
    old entry must be removed and a new one written, and between those two
    writes the name does not exist. That is a property of those formats, not of
    this implementation.

    mv x x is refused ("source and destination are the same").

SEE ALSO
